  'dns.title':            'DNS Record Monitoring',
  'dns.subtitle':         'Keeps an eye on your domains’ DNS records and alerts you when a value changes or a record stops resolving.',
  'dns.how1': 'From the monitoring server (single pod) it queries via dnsjava; the default path asks the system/OS DNS servers in order (primary→fallback). Each run resolves cache-free, so the response time is a real round-trip (DNS-slow detection). Query timeout is configurable (default 2000 ms). Direct UDP/TCP.',
  'dns.how2': 'Supported records: A, AAAA, CNAME, MX, TXT, NS, SOA, PTR, SRV, CAA. Success = NOERROR with a non-empty answer; sorted values, TTL (minimum) and response time are returned.',
  'dns.how3': 'Expected-value lock: any live value NOT in the expected set is flagged (rotation/subset is not a deviation; empty expected disables the lock). Change is classified NONE/ROTATED/CHANGED — a DNS-hijack signal.',
  'dns.how4': 'Propagation check: specific resolver IPs are queried individually; if at least 2 resolvers succeed but return different value sets it is marked "inconsistent" (split-DNS / propagation lag / poisoning signal).',
  'dns.how5': 'Where does the query go? The default path is the monitoring server\'s OS DNS chain (primary→fallback, in order; timeout configurable, default 2000 ms). Monitors with propagation check also query public resolvers (default 8.8.8.8 / 1.1.1.1 / 9.9.9.9) directly. The "Resolver Configuration" section in the detail modal shows the active chain.',
